---
.title = "Tune and benchmark LLMD",
.description = "Measure time to first token and generation throughput, then tune concurrency and KV-cache capacity.",
.date = .date("2026-11-18T00:00:00"),
.layout = "content.shtml",
---
Run the [LLMD quickstart]($link.page('llmd/quickstart')) first to verify that
your checkpoint loads and answers a request. The built-in benchmark starts its
own LLMD instance, sends a warm-up batch followed by a measured batch over
loopback, prints results, and exits. Run it on a test instance with an unused
listener port and no competing work on the selected devices.

The examples follow LLMD source revision `ca575167`. Benchmark flags are
omitted from that revision's `--help`. Use a build that supports them and
record its version. A local Metal run with Llama 3.2 1B confirmed TTFT and
throughput reporting. The LFM3 commands below are source-reviewed.

# [Run a baseline]($heading.id('run-a-baseline'))

Use the complete `LiquidAI/LFM3-1.7B-FP8` checkpoint under `${HOME}/models`.
The [quickstart]($link.page('llmd/quickstart').ref('checkpoint-storage'))
explains how to download and pin a checkpoint revision. This checkpoint has
published Metal and CUDA launch recipes in the
[model compatibility catalog]($link.page('llmd/models')).

On Apple Silicon, run the benchmark after installing LLMD with Homebrew.

```sh
llmd --model="${HOME}/models/LiquidAI/LFM3-1.7B-FP8" \
  --listen=127.0.0.1:18000 --batch-size=4 --max-context-len=4096 \
  --bench --bench-prompt-len=256 --bench-max-tokens=128 \
  --bench-concurrency=4 --bench-warmup-requests=4 \
  > llmd-bench-metal.log 2>&1
```

On a CUDA host configured as described in
[Deploy LLMD with Docker]($link.page('operations/deployment')), use the CUDA
image. The benchmark client runs inside the container, so it can use the
container's loopback address without a published host port.

```sh
docker pull zmlai/llmd:cuda &&
docker run --rm --gpus=all --shm-size=256GB \
  --mount "type=bind,source=${HOME}/models/LiquidAI/LFM3-1.7B-FP8,target=/model,readonly" \
  zmlai/llmd:cuda --model=/model \
  --listen=127.0.0.1:18000 --batch-size=4 --max-context-len=4096 \
  --bench --bench-prompt-len=256 --bench-max-tokens=128 \
  --bench-concurrency=4 --bench-warmup-requests=4 \
  > llmd-bench-cuda.log 2>&1
```

`--bench-prompt-len` counts words. The benchmark reports actual prompt tokens
after tokenization and varies the prompts to exercise prefill. Use
`--bench-prompt='Your prompt'` in place of `--bench-prompt-len` to repeat one
prompt and measure a workload that can reuse cached prefixes. The benchmark
ignores end-of-sequence tokens by default. Add `--bench-stop-on-eos` to allow
early stopping. Keep this choice consistent across runs.

# [Read and compare the results]($heading.id('read-and-compare-the-results'))

Open the log after the command exits and look for the benchmark summary. If
LLMD exits before printing it, check the log for a startup or request error.

| Log field | What it measures |
| --- | --- |
| `Prompt tokens` | Total tokenized input across the measured requests. |
| `TTFT` | Time to first token in milliseconds, including HTTP, tokenization, queueing, and prefill. |
| `Prefill throughput` | Prompt tokens per second from the first request until the last request receives its first token. |
| `Average throughput` or `Aggregate throughput` | Completion tokens per second over the streaming interval. The benchmark reports average throughput for one request and aggregate throughput for a batch. |

Record the LLMD version or image ID, checkpoint revision, device and driver,
flags, and log for each run. Change one setting at a time. For example, hold
`--batch-size=4` and the prompt and output sizes fixed while varying
`--bench-concurrency` between `1` and `4`. Use the same warm-up count and
repeat each case. Compare recorded TTFT and output throughput. A four-request
batch offers too few samples for a meaningful p99 latency estimate. Use a
sustained client workload for production latency measurements and inspect
[service metrics]($link.page('operations/monitoring').ref('scrape-service-metrics'))
alongside the results.

# [Tune context and concurrency]($heading.id('tune-context-and-concurrency'))

The KV cache holds attention state for tokens in active sequences. Longer
contexts and more simultaneous sequences increase the amount of state that may
need to stay in memory. `--max-context-len` caps tokens per sequence, including
the rendered prompt and generated output. `--batch-size` controls how many
requests the server can schedule together. `--bench-concurrency` controls how
many requests the benchmark submits together, so setting it above the server
batch size can introduce queueing.
The [CLI reference]($link.page('llmd/cli').ref('batching-context-and-memory'))
lists the accepted values and memory fractions.

In the reviewed source, LFM3 requires `--data-parallel=1`, the default. It
subtracts weights and other fixed allocations from its cache memory budget and
caps the resulting page count at the configured live batch's needs. With the
baseline's batch size of four, 4,096 tokens per sequence, and the default
16-token pages, that cap is 1,024 pages. Available memory can reduce it.

If startup runs out of memory, reduce the context or batch limit. If the
checkpoint weights exceed available memory, use a smaller checkpoint. If long
requests queue or exhaust the cache after startup, reduce concurrent load and
measure again. Change `--token-batch-size` or `--prefill-chunk-size` one at a
time to evaluate prompt-processing latency.
